The handshake field names were guessed. Meta's real protocol docs are now in docs/research/meta-voice/
(api-reference_voice_realtime.md, api-reference_voice_schemas.md, speech-to-text.md). Read them fully. Key points: send
the handshake as the first JSON text frame within 10 s (camelCase fields, e.g. "audioEncoding": "PCM_24KHZ"); the
endpoint does NOT read the HTTP Authorization header, the credential travels in the handshake; raw PCM binary frames;
end input with {"type":"endStream"} and read until close 1000; server "transcript" events with a "final" flag; "error"
events; close codes as documented; keyword biasing / endpointing / session limits per the schemas.
Do:
1. Rewrite the spike's handshake and the proxy's session_config() to the documented schema, including keyword biasing
   if the schema supports it, and the audio format the Android client sends (pick a documented encoding; change the
   Android AudioRecord rate to match, e.g. 24 kHz if PCM_24KHZ).
2. The key is inside the handshake frame: make sure it is never logged, never in fixtures, never in asr_segment
   events, never echoed to the client; test that with a fake upstream that records frames.
3. Add websockets to backend/pyproject.toml with a DECISIONS note (needed for the upstream WS client; FastAPI serves the
   client side).
4. Make the spike print each server event type and save key-free fixtures; I will rerun it live after you finish and
   you flip fixtures to live in a follow-up.
Run the backend suite (< 3 min), Android build (offline, seeded GRADLE_USER_HOME). Do not commit. Foreground only.
